WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.080
Lay a seedling on its side.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.660
Its shoot turns up, and its root turns down.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.040
Yet both gather auxin on their lower side.

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.400
How can one signal give opposite bends?

5
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:21.250
The first film showed light moving auxin to the shaded flank, which grew faster.

6
00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.030
Gravity moves auxin too.

7
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:26.970
Same gold dots, new direction.

8
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.830
This time, though, the shoot and the root disagree.

9
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:34.513
First, the plant must sense which way is down.

10
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:40.653
In the root cap, central cells called columella cells hold plastids packed with

11
00:00:40.653 --> 00:00:41.653
dense starch.

12
00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:47.167
Turn the root on its side, and these heavy plastids sink to the new floor of each

13
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:51.767
cell. They act as statoliths, one of the root's gravity sensors.

14
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.320
Auxin leaves cells through carriers called PIN proteins.

15
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.000
In Arabidopsis root-cap cells, one carrier, PIN3, moves quickly to the new lower

16
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.000
face.

17
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:07.253
So auxin flows toward the lower side of the root.

18
00:01:08.513 --> 00:01:11.853
In the shoot, extra auxin gathers along the lower flank too.

19
00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:15.127
You met the rule at the start of the lesson.

20
00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:17.667
Now apply it to the root.

21
00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:20.767
Auxin piles up on its lower flank.

22
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:24.767
Does that flank grow faster or slower than the top?

23
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:37.950
Slower. In the root, this auxin inhibits elongation of the lower epidermal cells.

24
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.070
The upper flank outgrows it, and the root curves down.

25
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:47.110
In the shoot, extra auxin speeds elongation of the lower flank.

26
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.470
So the shoot curves up.

27
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:51.993
Why opposite answers?

28
00:01:53.473 --> 00:01:55.833
Plot growth against auxin level for each organ.

29
00:01:57.193 --> 00:01:59.293
Each curve rises to a peak, then falls.

30
00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:03.617
Roots respond to much lower auxin levels than shoots.

31
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:06.757
So the root's curve sits far to the left.

32
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.880
Where does each organ work on its curve?

33
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.460
In the classic picture, a shoot works on its rising slope.

34
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.540
There, more auxin means faster growth.

35
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.310
A root works past its peak.

36
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.410
There, more auxin means slower growth.

37
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.130
So the same kind of signal gives opposite bends.

38
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:31.523
But these curves give the sign of each bend, not its cause.

39
00:02:32.943 --> 00:02:35.923
Each tissue turns auxin into growth in its own way.

40
00:02:36.943 --> 00:02:40.083
Why roots are so sensitive is still not fully explained.

41
00:02:41.217 --> 00:02:43.517
The plant is not reaching for anything.

42
00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:47.017
Light or gravity, the rule is the same.

43
00:02:48.177 --> 00:02:50.117
The plant moves auxin to one flank.

44
00:02:51.377 --> 00:02:54.437
That flank grows faster in a shoot, and slower in a root.
